18602, "top": [[".", -0.03013509139418602], [" because", -4.336400032043457]]}, {"t": "\n\n", "lp": -0.00013042346108704805, "top": [["\n\n", -0.00013042346108704805], [" The", -11.415575981140137]]}, {"t": "---", "lp": -0.2886256277561188, "top": [["---", -0.2886256277561188], ["###", -1.386468768119812]]}, {"t": "\n\n", "lp": -2.30076584557537e-05, "top": [["\n\n", -2.30076584557537e-05], ["", -11.926772117614746]]}, {"t": "###", "lp": -7.927732076495886e-05, "top": [["###", -7.927732076495886e-05], ["2", -11.079497337341309]]}], "text": "Based on current technical documentation and driver behavior for Intel Arc GPUs (including the A770) under the Linux `xe` driver (or the newer `intel-mesa`/`i915` drivers), here is the breakdown:\n\n### 1. **CCS Mode 1 vs. Mode 2**\n\n**CCS (Constant Color Surface)** modes refer to how the GPU handles **color space conversions** and **gamma correction** during rendering, particularly when using **YUV** or **HDR** content.\n\n- **CCS Mode 1**:  \n  - **Standard sRGB / Linear workflow**.  \n  - Used for **SDR (Standard Dynamic Range)** content.  \n  - Applies standard gamma correction (typically 2.2 or sRGB curve).  \n  - **Default mode** for most desktop compositing and video playback.  \n  - **Use when**: You are displaying normal SDR content (e.g., YouTube videos, desktop UI, games in SDR mode).\n\n- **CCS Mode 2**:  \n  - **HDR (High Dynamic Range) workflow**.  \n  - Enables **linear color space** handling with **extended dynamic range**.  \n  - Requires the display and compositor to support HDR (e.g., Wayland with HDR support, or specific video players like `mpv` with HDR shaders).  \n  - **Use when**: You are playing HDR video content (e.g., 4K HDR Blu-rays, HDR YouTube videos) and want accurate tone-mapping and brightness preservation.\n\n> **Note**: The Intel Arc A770 supports HDR output via the `xe` driver, but **CCS Mode 2 is only effective if your display and compositor support HDR**. If you set CCS Mode 2 on an SDR display, you may see washed-out or incorrect colors.\n\n---\n\n###"}